<RecruiterLayout
  title="DM drugstores / consumer attitudes research"
  description="Survey research into how consumers reacted when DM replaced discounts and promotions with permanent low pricing. 258 respondents, analysed in SPSS."
  heading="dm drogerije."
  course="Applied Marketing Research, MSc"
  year="2022"
  scope="individual project"
  tools={['SPSS', 'Survey design', 'Reliability analysis', 'Pearson correlation', 't-test', 'ANOVA', 'Factor analysis']}
>

  <h2>the brief</h2>

  <p>
    DM drugstores moved away from running discounts and promotions and towards a permanently lower price programme
    called <em>UVEK POVOLJNO</em>. That is a significant change to make to a customer base that has been trained for
    years to wait for a sale. The question was whether shoppers actually accepted it, and whether it changed what
    they do.
  </p>

  <div class="rc-stats">
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">258</span><span class="rc-stat-l">respondents</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">17</span><span class="rc-stat-l">question instrument</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">0.838</span><span class="rc-stat-l">Cronbach's alpha, main scale</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>approach</h2>

  <h3>Instrument and sample</h3>
  <p>
    A 17-question survey, analysed in SPSS. The sample split into three age bands: 17 to 24 (92 respondents, 35.7%),
    25 to 28 (83, 32.2%) and 29 to 70 (83, 32.2%).
  </p>
  <p>
    Cleaning came first. 44 respondents turned out not to know the programme at all, so their answers to
    programme-specific questions were excluded rather than treated as opinions. It is a small thing, but leaving
    them in would have quietly diluted every result that followed.
  </p>

  <h3>Checking the scales before trusting them</h3>
  <p>
    I ran reliability analysis before any of the interesting tests. The main attitude scale returned a Cronbach's
    alpha of <strong>0.838</strong>, comfortably above the 0.7 threshold, and the item-total correlations showed no
    item worth removing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three other scales did not reach acceptable reliability. Those were not analysed further. Reporting that is less
    satisfying than presenting five more charts, but a scale that is not internally consistent does not measure
    anything you can act on.
  </p>

  <h3>Testing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pearson correlation</strong> between attitude variables, interpreted against conventional weak, moderate and strong bands. The strongest relationship found was r = 0.653.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent samples t-test</strong> comparing two groups. The result was not significant, p = 0.096, with badly unequal group sizes (190 against 24), so I reported it as inconclusive rather than reading a trend into it.</li>
    <li><strong>One-way ANOVA</strong> across the three age bands, to see whether attitude to the programme varied by age.</li>
    <li><strong>Factor analysis</strong>, preceded by KMO and sphericity checks to confirm the data was suitable for it in the first place.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>what came out of it</h2>

  <p>
    Discounting still shapes behaviour, and not gently. <strong>51%</strong> of respondents said they stockpile
    products when something is discounted, against 41.6% who do not. Given a choice of mechanics, a straight
    <strong>50% off</strong> was preferred by 48.8%, with 1+1 free second at 20.9% and a combination of the two at
    18.1%. Only 4% chose "50% off when you buy two".
  </p>

  <div class="rc-result">
    <p class="rc-result-label">the practical read</p>
    <p>
      A permanently lower shelf price is rational, but it removes the event that a large share of customers were
      organising their shopping around. The recommendation was to keep the pricing change and give that behaviour
      somewhere else to go: a loyalty mechanic and time-bound, gamified offers that create moments to respond to
      without returning to blanket discounting.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="rc-note">
    This is the project where I learned most about being disciplined with results. The temptation with survey data
    is to present everything you ran. Dropping three unreliable scales and reporting a non-significant t-test as
    non-significant left a shorter piece of work, and a defensible one.
  </p>

</RecruiterLayout>
